--TEST--
`put` over a key that already holds a value replaces it — the read after every write in a sweep answers that write, the shape may change with it, and thirteen rewrites totalling twice the cap forget nothing that was written before them — `rule:core-api/two-cache-tiers`, `rule:concurrency/a-cached-value-is-copied-across-the-boundary` and `rule:concurrency/cache-memory-is-charged-to-the-core`
--FILE--
<?nvs
// § 2 gives the store two operations and no third one, so there is no spelling
// of an append, a merge or an add-if-absent: a `put` over a live key is the
// whole of what happens to that key. The sweep below is what a store keeping
// the *first* write, or holding a longer value and overwriting it in place,
// would fail while answering plausibly on any single line.
var $cache = Core\Cache::local();

// Written before every rewrite, so it is the oldest key in the tier and the
// first one the cap would forget.  Nothing below names it again.
$cache->put("steady", "written once, before all of it");

// The sweep alternates the length in both directions and writes a value it has
// already written: a store truncating to the incoming length would pass the
// growing half, one padding to the length it held would pass the shrinking
// half, and one treating the empty string as an absence would pass all but two.
array<string> $values = [
    "first",
    "",
    Core\Str::repeat("long ", 28),
    "short again",
    Core\Str::repeat("x", 200),
    Core\Str::repeat("ab", 60),
    "first",
    Core\Str::repeat("y", 180),
    "",
    Core\Str::repeat("z", 150),
    Core\Str::repeat("mid ", 40),
    Core\Str::repeat("q", 120),
    "the last one",
];

tainted string $back = "";
int $answered = 0;
foreach ($values as string $value) {
    $cache->put("hot", $value);
    $back = $cache->get("hot") as string;
    if ($back == $value) {
        $answered = $answered + 1;
    }
}
echo "the read after each write answered that write: ", $answered, " of ", Core\Arr::count($values), "\n";

// The replacement is of the entry and not of its bytes, so the shape a key
// holds is the shape of the last value put there — § 2's copy runs over
// whatever arrived, with nothing of the key's previous payload in it.
$cache->put("shape", "text first");
$cache->put("shape", 7);
int $flipped = $cache->get("shape") as int;
echo "a rewrite replaces the shape as well: ", $flipped, "\n";

// And a rewrite costs the tier one entry rather than one per write. Those
// thirteen writes total more than twice the cap below while the largest two of
// them fit under it together, so a store that added an entry per write would
// have had to forget its oldest key — and the oldest key is the one written
// before the sweep started. `crates/nvs-stdlib/src/cache.rs`'s `order` queue is
// where that is decided: a slot per live key, in the order each was **first**
// written, so an overwrite keeps its place and leaves nothing behind it.
string $steady = $cache->get("steady") == null ? "forgotten" : "kept";
echo "the key written before every one of those rewrites was ", $steady, "\n";
--FILE nvs.toml--
# § 3's cap, set low enough that the sweep's own bytes would not fit under it
# twice and high enough that the two largest entries fit side by side — which is
# what an overwrite needs, since the cap prices the incoming entry before it
# takes out the one being replaced.
[cache.local]
max_size = "1K"
--EXPECT--
the read after each write answered that write: 13 of 13
a rewrite replaces the shape as well: 7
the key written before every one of those rewrites was kept
